super heavy rich  foods morning noon and night every  single day even if i wasn't even in the  mood for them i'd feel totally tapped  out but i felt like i had to i had no  choice i would basically just be going  through the motions of a binge just  because i knew that i had to get the bad  foods in while i could but here in pei  we would often get some kind of fried  fish or like a lobster roll for lunch  and it would always come with fries but  after having like a piece of battered  fish or really buttery lobster roll i  often wouldn't even want more than a few  fries now i know that this takes a lot  of practice but i've realized that a  handful of fries a few times a week  tastes so much better than a mountain of  fries once a week ditto for dessert  alcohol and even stereotypically  quote-unquote healthy foods as well  moderation and variety will always yield  the most physically and emotionally  satisfying experience moving on to tip 5  when you make a food choice ask yourself  how does this food make me feel so my  approach to food on a holiday today is  so different than it was 10 years ago  when i was really struggling with food i  would restrict really heavily leading up  to kind of a holiday so that i could  binge on all of these heavy over-the-top  meals breakfast lunch and dinner seven  days a week only to get home and feel  really unwell physically and also  riddled with guilt emotionally which of  course would just then start the cycle  all over again and it wouldn't be hard  for me to let my anxiety push me back  into that same cycle again sure i  probably ate more fried foods than i  normally do but i actually felt really  great physically because  i never felt the need to overeat if i  ate fish and chips for lunch i would  naturally choose some kind of steamed  seafood with like a salad and some corn  for dinner at home it was never an  intentional oh i had a cheat meal now i  need to eat super clean it was truly  just an intuitive way to eat and feel my  body ditto after my one big meal at the  inn  so last night was so much fun first of  all just nice to talk to other human  beings which we haven't gotten a lot of  exposure to in ontario um and also just  like an amazing experience they did a  farm tour and the farm is amazing then  like a little oyster hour and they have  tacos and cocktails and we had so much  fun and honestly i was talking to my  husband afterwards i was like that was  so awesome  and so much food like we had all the  kind of hors d'oeuvres and then the sit  down multi-course meal  and i was thinking and talking to him  and i was like do you remember the time  when we were like 10 years younger and  we used to do like back-to-back tasting  menus with wine pairings when we  traveled  now i'm like  just give me like i just want a fruit  salad for breakfast after that because i  still felt quite satisfied when i woke  up um but the beautiful thing about food  freedom is that i was able to listen to  my body and not like force myself  through finishing a course when i just  like wasn't for it though i will say i  did eat the entire dessert course  because hey we got a second stomach for  dessert right um but by the time that  the main came around i maybe ate like  maybe a third of it as delicious as it  was i just  couldn't put it back and i lost my  appetite and i was able to just kind of  say i'll take this home for the kids and  that's exactly what we did but anyways  it was a fantastic experience if you're  ever in pei that is absolutely something  that you want to book ahead on they book  way in advance  so definitely take the time to look that  up in at bay fortune with chef michael  smith and try to get in their books oh  i'm sad that this holiday is ending  but alas  all good things have to come to an end  and i need to get back to work so  tis life  now obviously this takes practice and  time to really be able to trust  ourselves not to overeat especially if  you're used to restricting all of your  adult life but remind yourself tomorrow  is another delicious day there's no need  to eat to the point of discomfort when  your body trusts itself that there's  something delicious to be had again  really soon and finally tip number six  embrace the ena i think this has been  the hardest thing for me to learn  because you know relaxing or being  enough or doing the bare minimum doesn't  come easy to me i'm a type a  perfectionist people pleaser so there's  always something that i feel that i need  to do now at times in my life this has  bled into my relationship with food  exercise and my body and i'm always  going to have to work to challenge this  desire to do more but your body and mind  need rest  my body and my mind needs rest and  nothing bad is going to happen if we  take it so what i might not eat as many  vegetables and my workouts might not be  as intense while i'm on vacation but the  point is that i'm gonna do what feels  good to me and what is easily accessible  to me because that's probably a lot much  healthier thing to do than to obsess  over getting my heart rate up or getting  in a certain number of steps on my  holiday for me i know that starting my  day with a little bit of movement makes  me feel good no matter where i am now at  home i have a pretty extensive at-home  gym set up with like a treadmill and  free weights and a functional cables  machine here  i had a floor with a beach towel and it  probably was like 45 minutes away to the  nearest public gym so that was not even  an option so i brought a few easy  lightweight pieces of equipment just for  general stretching and some basic  exercises to help with my diastasis  recti and to just like give me that me  time in the day without letting it get  in the way of other fun things that we  had planned i try to move my body in  ways that feels good and it does feel  good for me to try to move my body every  day um so if i can fit it in great if i  can't it's not the end of the world  things like walking on the beach and  chasing after my kids was really the  main activity that i did most days and i  didn't freak out and only eat salad on  the rainy days that we spent mainly  indoors or in the car and you know what  nothing bad happened i didn't come home  completely deconditioned because of it i  actually felt better in tune with my  body and what felt good to it because  again there was no expectation there was  no routine no noise just an open space  for me to tune in and listen to my  desire to maybe like go for an early  sunrise walk or have the energy to fully  invest in family time it was bliss now  listen i know that we can't all just  take a vacation anytime we have a  stressful week at work  this is actually my first actual  vacation that i've taken since before i  had kids like i didn't even take a mat  leaf with either kid not that anything  about a mat leaf is like a relaxing  holiday trust me but anyways i'm just  saying i've never  really taken the time to slow down and  while i'm often around with my kids i  usually feel a lot of guilt that i'm  just not present nearly enough and as a  result my mental health definitely  suffers i don't know maybe you guys can  relate so if you're finding your  day-to-day grind is causing stress that  triggers your urge to control maybe some  of these little tips can actually help i  know that i'm going to be using them a  lot myself as i try to manage my own  mental health struggles as we enter  another uncertain unfortunate season of  coveted life but on that note that is  all that i have for you guys today so  thank you again to squarespace for  sponsoring this video if you liked it be  sure to give it the thumbs up leave me a  comment below if you want to see more  vloggy stuff if you have any fun topics  you want me to cover things you want me  to talk about let me know and of course  i'll see you again next time on abby's  kitchen  bye
 

